OCGRAPH 1
# A THING THAT SCORES. Placed by the map, hit by the player's ray, counts what it receives.
#
# BECOMING SHOOTABLE -- the reason this is `Character` and not `Actor`, which looks wrong until you
# need it. A `class=` placement gets NO physics body from anywhere in this engine: LevelInstance's
# static-box path (the one that stamps a level placement's body with its entity) is deliberately
# skipped for class placements, and nothing in ClassBuilder can add a collider. So a plain Actor target
# is invisible to a raycast -- the ray passes straight through the thing you can plainly see. Character
# is the one class whose graph-facing node reaches a real Jolt body: AverCharacter.EnsureCapsule,
# reached through CharacterMove, which also stamps that capsule with its owning entity the instant it
# exists. (A CharacterVirtual used to be invisible to queries even then, because Jolt does not put one
# in the broadphase; aver_phys_character_create now gives every character a real inner body.)
#
# ONE CharacterMove CALL, AT OnStart, EVERY MOTION INPUT ZERO. EnsureCapsule is lazy -- first call
# creates it -- so this has to run once for the target to exist physically at all, and never again.
# Wiring it into OnTick would re-walk the same zero-motion command every frame for nothing.
#
# TWO HONEST CONSEQUENCES, NAMED RATHER THAN HIDDEN. First: these targets STAY WHERE THE MAP PUTS
# THEM. A CharacterVirtual is only integrated when something drives it, and this graph drives it once
# at OnStart and never again -- so a target hangs at its placement height instead of falling to the
# floor. That is convenient here (a target at eye height is what you want on a range) but it is a
# consequence of not ticking the character, NOT of anything asking it to hover, and a target you give
# an OnTick CharacterMove to will start falling. Second:
# AverCharacter.Drive unconditionally publishes a GLOBAL "camera follows this entity" request, so each
# target briefly asks for the camera as its capsule is made. The player's own CharacterMove runs every
# OnTick and reclaims it immediately, so the visible effect is at most one frame at startup -- but it
# is a real shared-global seam, not a thing this file is pretending away.
NAME FPTarget
DESCRIPTION Placed in the level, shootable, counts the hits it receives as its own score.

CLASS AN_FPTarget Character mesh=Meshes/sphere.ocmesh material=M_Target

PARAM entity int
PARAM deltaTime float

# ---- CANVAS REGIONS ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Three entry points, three boxes. OnStart, OnHit and OnTick are independent chains that share
# only the score VAR, and stacking them in one column is what hid that. No wiring changed.
#
# COMMENT id x y w h r g b text...
COMMENT inputs 0 0 400 308 58 64 78 INPUTS  -  entity, deltaTime
COMMENT settle 0 458 1120 600 52 74 96 ON START  -  scale up, settle once
COMMENT score 0 1208 1120 394 86 58 62 ON HIT  -  this target owns its own score
COMMENT report 0 1752 400 308 58 64 78 ON TICK  -  OUT the score

NODE ent Param 40 110 param=entity
NODE dt Param 40 196 param=deltaTime

VAR score float 0

ENTRY seed OnStart
NODE seed OnStart 40 568

# ---- make the capsule, so a ray can find this at all ------------------------------------------------
NODE zero ConstFloat 40 654 value=0.0

NODE settle CharacterMove 400 568
LINK seed.exec settle.exec
LINK ent.value settle.entity
LINK dt.value settle.dt
LINK zero.value settle.forward
LINK zero.value settle.right
LINK zero.value settle.yawDelta
LINK zero.value settle.pitchDelta

# ---- self-sizing ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The built-in sphere is generated at radius 1cm, and a 1cm dot is not a target anyone can see or aim
# at. There is no placement-time scale for a class instance, so a SetFieldVec3 write at OnStart is the
# only lever this vocabulary has.
#
# 25, AND THE NUMBER IS LOAD-BEARING FOR MORE THAN LOOKS. The sphere is centred on the entity ORIGIN,
# which for a Character is its FEET, while the capsule a ray actually hits spans feet..feet+180
# (Height). The two can never share a centre without a child transform, so what matters is that the
# ball sits somewhere a person would aim: at 25 it spans feet-25..feet+25, and with these targets
# placed at z=100 that is 75..125, comfortably in view and inside the capsule that is actually hit.
#
# IT WAS 90, AND 90 BROKE THE RENDERER. Three 1.8-METRE saturated-red spheres under a 100,000-lux sun
# bounce an enormous amount of red light, and with ray-traced sun shadows ON -- which light them more
# brightly than the voxel-cone path does -- the global illumination ran away and flooded the entire
# viewport red, on any hardware, at any resolution. Bisected to exactly this: remove the targets and it
# renders; drop them to scale 20 and it renders; leave them at 90 and it floods. That the engine
# degrades this badly rather than clamping is its own defect and is tracked separately -- but a target
# the size of a person was never the right look, and the fix here is the honest one either way.
#
# SetFieldVec3 HAS NO EXEC PINS BY DEFAULT, and this graph compiles PUSH (it has three ENTRY records),
# where a side-effecting node with no incoming exec edge is refused rather than pulled. Reaching it
# from OnStart therefore means declaring every one of its seven pins by hand -- any PIN record at all
# suppresses ALL defaults for that node, so miss one and it is simply gone, not defaulted.
NODE sx ConstFloat 400 774 value=25.0
NODE sy ConstFloat 400 860 value=25.0
NODE sz ConstFloat 400 946 value=25.0

NODE grow SetFieldVec3 760 568 field=CLocal.scale
PIN grow exec in exec
PIN grow entity in int
PIN grow x in float
PIN grow y in float
PIN grow z in float
PIN grow then out exec
PIN grow success out bool
LINK settle.then grow.exec
LINK ent.value grow.entity
LINK sx.value grow.x
LINK sy.value grow.y
LINK sz.value grow.z

# ---- the reaction -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# An ENTRY is not a node type: it names which node begins a chain. OnHit differs from OnStart and
# OnTick only in that nobody calls it for you -- another graph's FireEvent does.
ENTRY hit OnHit
NODE hit OnHit 40 1318

NODE one ConstFloat 40 1404 value=1.0

NODE prev GetVar 40 1490 var=score
NODE next Add 400 1318
LINK prev.value next.a
LINK one.value next.b

NODE store SetVar 760 1318 var=score
LINK hit.exec store.exec
LINK next.result store.value

# ---- reporting --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A SEPARATE GetVar from `prev`: pulling `prev` here would report the score as it stood BEFORE this
# tick's hit was stored -- an off-by-one that reads as a lost point.
ENTRY tick OnTick
NODE tick OnTick 40 1862
NODE report GetVar 40 1948 var=score

OUT report value
